[{:db/id 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" "question-93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" :question/id #uuid "0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" :question/math-academy-id "q-88693" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e7444ba-e9b0-5dc3-868c-f11ef347b50d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e7444ba-e9b0-5dc3-868c-f11ef347b50d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e7444ba-e9b0-5dc3-868c-f11ef347b50d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a3cfc328-4fc1-5747-b490-dfadf05a9a5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df465832-9958-51d8-a73c-05aadf6adf21" "answer-47aae119-de52-5a6f-b7a1-ed5cfb5a10e2" "answer-a66e2889-63d9-58f5-b599-fac84f95bb5f" "answer-7c9326e8-8412-5f0f-be79-a57ba80e957f" "answer-a3cfc328-4fc1-5747-b490-dfadf05a9a5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df465832-9958-51d8-a73c-05aadf6adf21" :answer/id #uuid "df465832-9958-51d8-a73c-05aadf6adf21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47aae119-de52-5a6f-b7a1-ed5cfb5a10e2" :answer/id #uuid "47aae119-de52-5a6f-b7a1-ed5cfb5a10e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a66e2889-63d9-58f5-b599-fac84f95bb5f" :answer/id #uuid "a66e2889-63d9-58f5-b599-fac84f95bb5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c9326e8-8412-5f0f-be79-a57ba80e957f" :answer/id #uuid "7c9326e8-8412-5f0f-be79-a57ba80e957f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3cfc328-4fc1-5747-b490-dfadf05a9a5b" :answer/id #uuid "a3cfc328-4fc1-5747-b490-dfadf05a9a5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" :question/id #uuid "93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115272" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93e076d5-a5ae-573d-9e84-8303573a1f36"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93e076d5-a5ae-573d-9e84-8303573a1f36" :answer-field/id #uuid "93e076d5-a5ae-573d-9e84-8303573a1f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-376dd84b-186c-5a4f-8a0a-85f3f4cc251e" "answer-761e54dd-814f-565c-bb2b-669fb57d2836" "answer-130e6afa-dce0-5b08-819c-ef94704234fd" "answer-5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" "answer-f5c2246d-16ff-5312-93dd-63dfdff73262"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-376dd84b-186c-5a4f-8a0a-85f3f4cc251e" :answer/id #uuid "376dd84b-186c-5a4f-8a0a-85f3f4cc251e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-761e54dd-814f-565c-bb2b-669fb57d2836" :answer/id #uuid "761e54dd-814f-565c-bb2b-669fb57d2836" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-130e6afa-dce0-5b08-819c-ef94704234fd" :answer/id #uuid "130e6afa-dce0-5b08-819c-ef94704234fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" :answer/id #uuid "5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5c2246d-16ff-5312-93dd-63dfdff73262" :answer/id #uuid "f5c2246d-16ff-5312-93dd-63dfdff73262" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157398 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" "question-7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" :question/id #uuid "c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-334355" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\sqrt{4-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ad199620-f508-5a84-91ee-7b219e7a24f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad199620-f508-5a84-91ee-7b219e7a24f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad199620-f508-5a84-91ee-7b219e7a24f2" :answer/id #uuid "ad199620-f508-5a84-91ee-7b219e7a24f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053" :question/id #uuid "7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053" :question/math-academy-id "q-334371" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b770ccd-99d1-5c98-a430-d44955178ce6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b770ccd-99d1-5c98-a430-d44955178ce6" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b770ccd-99d1-5c98-a430-d44955178ce6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d" :answer/id #uuid "8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-p" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-dd7f20dd-b98f-586f-b688-7ce1c3a853b4" :activity/id #uuid "dd7f20dd-b98f-586f-b688-7ce1c3a853b4" :activity/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048495 :activity/